A||||20||SCCA FIERO||88 coupe and 88 GT suspension exactly the same?
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||05-17-99||09:03 PM||||Are the GTs and Formulas equipped with something the coupes aren't? I think the sway bars are different but anything else? I'm thinking about buying an 88 coupe just for the suspension. My 86 SE is fun to autocross but it's a handful! I would plan to put a V6 in this 88 coupe to give it the attitude it deserves but if the suspension is different I may think a little harder before i buy it. I can get an 88 coupe with an automatic and 139,000 on the 4 cylinder mill for $1350. Is it worth it to you? It needs paint and I have an extra roof skin to add a sunroof and i would add my factory spoiler from my SE. Then i would spray it yellow! I guess i would pretty much have a Formula. Then maybe a 3.1 crank and a nice cam and if my wife would let me i would get a turbo. Then.....blah blah blah......
Z||000001||Carrolles||05-17-99||10:24 PM||||I believe all 88 GTs have the WS6 suspension and they all have a rear sway bar and different springs.  All 88 coupes have the 4 cyl engine and probably have different springs and sway bars.<br>The general suspension arangement (control arms, steering rack, geometry, etc) is the same.<p>If the car is in decent shape I'd buy it.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||05-18-99||12:16 AM||||I think i am gonna buy it! Everything on the 88 that needs to be replaced/repaired is good on my car. 
Z||000003||ChadMan||05-18-99||07:14 AM||||I have a 5-speed '88 Coupe and absolutely love it.  A fellow at the Fiero Store told me the only differences between my suspension and the GT/Formulas are the rear swaybar (Coupes have none) and the rear spring rates (higher in the GT/Formula to accommodate the V6).  <p>I swapped my rear springs for Formula springs and replaced the worn-out factory shocks/struts with KYB's.  My 88 handles great, but what really sets it apart from my 86 SE is braking.  The 88 setup has a much firmer pedal and is easier to modulate with minimal brake fade.<p>I am thinking of autocrossing next summer, and will almost definitely use the 88.
Z||000004||Carrolles||05-18-99||07:29 AM||||That's right.  I forgot that 88s have vented disks.  All others have solid disks.
Z||000005||Carrolles||05-18-99||07:31 AM||||That's right.  I forgot that 88s have vented disks.  All others have solid disks.
Z||000006||Carrolles||05-18-99||07:31 AM||||Oops!!  How'd that happen?
Z||000007||Eric||05-19-99||01:04 PM||||Carroll,<br>The 88s have 10.2 inch vented disks on all corners.  The earlier Fieros have vented disks up front and solid in the back.  Maybe that's what you meant to say.  I don't know.
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-19-99||04:16 PM||||I thought the 84 - 87 had non-vented disks at all 4 corners and that they were only about 9-1/2".
Z||000009||Eric||05-19-99||05:16 PM||||Well, I couldn't get an answer from my Haynes manual.  They had pictures of brake rotors all over the place, some vented some not.  Anybody else out there know for sure?  Look through the outside of your wheel.  I just can't imagine there are cars running around the streets without vented front rotors.  I guess you have to learn to use them once and no more than once. 
Z||000010||Phil||05-19-99||05:46 PM||||My 85GT has/had nonvented on all corners- my 88GT has vented all around
