Z||000019||Eric||06-12-99||01:52 PM||||Ross, they are the same.  The Formula is a little lighter so it performs a little better.
Z||000020||mrfiero||06-14-99||05:37 AM||||I'm relatively new.....I've been here for maybe 2 months.  I don't get online much so I don't normally post anything.  <p>About the '88 Formulas & GTs.....besides cosmetics and the various options they are identical cars.....identical engines/trans/suspensions/brake, etc. etc.    Performancewise the Formula has a slight advantage over the GT due to weighing approximately 200 pounds less (depending on how each are optioned.<p>I love my GT, but think my 5-sp. Formula is more fun!<p>Besides the spring rate and lack of a rear sway bar the '88 coupes have identical suspensions/brakes as the '88 Formulas and GTs.  The Formula was actually a $2000 option on the coupe.<p><br>Back to the rear sway bar.....I believe the factory bar is 7/8" in diameter.....not positive, but pretty sure.  There are aftermarket ones that are about 1 1/4", but I have no experience with them.  Findinf used rear sway bars is kind of difficult, but there is a company called RYANE Motorsports in Oregon who sells reproduction '88 rear sway bar strut brackets.....you could then buy an aftermarket bar and some endlinks and have what you need to install it on your coupe.  <br>
